# Implementation notes

The supplied specification is `interface.md`, supplemented in the separate repair
phase by the counterexamples in `feedback.json`. This implementation uses the Go
standard library and implements all four operations without external modules.

Build with `go build -o rosetta-jsonl main.go`. Run `./rosetta-jsonl` with one
request per stdin line. Test with `GO111MODULE=off go test .`.

## Assumptions where the interface is silent

- Transaction encoding uses little-endian fixed-width integers and canonical
  CompactSize counts and byte-string lengths. Version bits, previous indices,
  sequences, and locktime are unsigned 32-bit values. Repair feedback establishes
  that amounts are signed; they use signed 64-bit decimal values and preserve the
  corresponding two's-complement wire bits. No monetary, script, or transaction
  consensus-validity checks apply.
- Witness mode accepts ordinary legacy transactions and marker/flag witness
  transactions. Only flag 1 is supported. Witness framing with every input's
  witness stack empty is malformed. A stack containing an empty item is nonempty.
  Serialization includes witness framing only when requested and a nonempty
  witness stack exists; every input then receives its own witness stack encoding.
- In witness decoding, an initial zero input count followed by zero denotes an
  empty legacy transaction. A nonzero following byte is a witness flag. Thus
  legacy transactions with zero inputs and nonzero outputs should use legacy
  decoding mode; the same bytes are ambiguous in witness mode.
- `identify` reports stripped and witness-inclusive serialization. Both identifiers
  are double SHA-256 digests; display order reverses the 32 digest bytes. Without
  witness data, full and stripped bytes and their identifiers are identical.
- Prefix `consumed` is relative to the requested offset. Offsets beyond the byte
  buffer are invalid requests. An offset at its end gives malformed encoding.
  Exact decoding rejects any trailing bytes.
- `max_items` independently bounds input and output counts, each witness stack's
  item count, and each script or witness item's byte length. It does not bound
  fixed-width hashes, the entire encoded transaction, or an aggregate item total.
  It applies to all transaction data supplied to serialization or identification,
  including witness data when stripped serialization is requested. If omitted,
  there is no additional configured item limit. Wire counts are checked against
  remaining bytes before allocation and before the configured limit. If even
  the minimum encoded space required for a count cannot fit, the result is
  `malformed_encoding`, including when that count also exceeds `max_items`.
- Required fields must be present and of the documented JSON type; null does not
  substitute for an object, array, string, or boolean. Decimal strings contain
  only digits, except that amounts may have a leading minus sign, and must fit
  their field's range. Leading zeros and negative zero amounts are accepted.
  Hexadecimal is case-insensitive, must have even length, and has no `0x` prefix.
  Output hex and decimal strings are normalized. Extra object fields are ignored;
  duplicate object keys use the last value, as in Go's JSON decoder.
- Missing fields, malformed JSON/hex/numeric strings, and unsupported operations
  or modes give `invalid_request`. Invalid, truncated, noncanonical, or trailing
  wire data gives `malformed_encoding`. Exceeding a configured field limit gives
  `resource_limit` after the count's minimum encoded space has been checked.
  Unexpected recovered panics give `execution_failure`.
- No transport or admission thresholds were supplied, so the program invents
  neither and does not ordinarily emit `transport_limit` or `admission_limit`.
  JSON lines are not restricted to the default scanner's 64 KiB maximum. Blank
  lines are invalid requests; a final nonempty line without a newline is handled.

## Local verification

Tests cover manually assembled legacy/witness bytes, all operation response
shapes, digest and display ordering, exact/prefix boundaries, integer/count
boundaries, malformed/truncated wire data, invalid JSON shapes, configured limits,
large JSON lines, and 300 deterministic randomized round trips. A fuzz target
checks that every accepted wire prefix reserializes identically.

Repair regressions exercise every supplied counterexample through the JSON-lines
entry point and compare the entire response. Additional cases cover signed
64-bit amount boundaries, out-of-range amounts, and malformed-versus-resource
precedence for input/output counts, scripts, witness counts, and witness items.
Other assumptions above remain unconfirmed where the feedback is silent.
